{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST locate its per-user configuration at\n`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/habeas/config.toml` on Unix when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is an absolute,\nnonempty path, otherwise at `$HOME/.config/habeas/config.toml`. On Windows it MUST\nuse `%APPDATA%/habeas/config.toml`. `HOME` and `APPDATA` MUST likewise be\nabsolute, nonempty paths to be used. If no usable base directory can be\ndetermined, an operation requiring user configuration MUST fail with an\nexplanatory diagnostic naming the variable.\n\nThe same user configuration MUST be usable across projects. Its location MUST\nNOT be selected relative to the current project or changed by project settings.\nUser-local preferences for a project MAY remain in the user configuration;\nworkspace settings MUST use `habeas.toml` under\n{cite}`requirements.configuration.workspace_location`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I found no gaps. Every obligation in `requirements.configuration.user_location` has marked code and a test that asserts its outcome.\n\n**Obligations and code**\n- **Unix, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` used when absolute and nonempty:** `user_config_path_from` (`src/config.rs:688`) calls `absolute_directory` (`src/config.rs:724`). That helper keeps a value only if `PathBuf::is_absolute` holds, which an empty string never does. The result is joined with `habeas/config.toml`.\n- **Unix fallback to `$HOME/.config/habeas/config.toml`:** the `None` arm of the same match uses `required_directory(HOME)?.join(\".config\")`.\n- **Windows uses `%APPDATA%/habeas/config.toml`:** the `ConfigPlatform::Windows` arm uses `required_directory(\"APPDATA\")`. `ConfigPlatform::current()` (`src/config.rs:303`) and `user_config_path()` (`src/config.rs:683`) pick the platform and read the environment.\n- **`HOME` and `APPDATA` must be absolute and nonempty:** `required_directory` (`src/config.rs:732`) rejects any other value.\n- **Failure with a diagnostic naming the variable:** `required_directory` returns a `NotFound` error reading \"cannot determine Habeas directory: {name} must be an absolute, nonempty path\". `resolve_authoring` (`src/config.rs:960`) propagates it with `?`, so the operation fails with that message. `is_configuration_file` in `src/redaction.rs` calls `user_config_path().ok()` only to recognise the file, not to require it.\n- **Same configuration across projects, location not project-relative or settable by project settings:** `user_config_path_from` takes only a platform and an environment lookup, with no project or workspace input. The only callers I found are `resolve_authoring`, `is_configuration_file` and a test helper, and none passes project data.\n- **Workspace settings use `habeas.toml`:** this only cites `workspace_location`. That requirement's own code is not reassessed here.\n\n**Tests**\n- **`locations_use_absolute_xdg_values_and_platform_fallbacks`** (`tests/configuration.rs:12`) covers these cases:\n  - a valid `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is used;\n  - empty and relative `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` fall back to `HOME/.config`;\n  - the Windows `APPDATA` path;\n  - an error when nothing is set.\n- **`base_directories_must_be_absolute_and_nonempty`** (`tests/configuration.rs:1427`) covers these cases:\n  - empty and relative `HOME` and `APPDATA` fail, and the error text contains the variable name;\n  - a relative `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` falls back to an absolute `HOME`.\n- The `?` propagation in `resolve_authoring` is a pass-through with no conditional, so it needs no test of its own.\n- The \"not project-relative\" obligation holds by construction, because the function takes no project input.\n\n**Unspecified behaviour:** I found none. The `ConfigPlatform` enum and the `required_directory` helper are the implementation of the stated rules.\n\n**Marking:** every implementing item has an `{impl}` reference and both tests have `{test}` references. I found no unmarked code or tests.",
  "references": "a2800054065cedb39cfbad2b80c0a042d1159e4e7bdddddd8ac9e49a6e551787",
